<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nabonidus Chronicle</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabonidus">Nabonidus</a> Chronicle is an ancient Babylonian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> text recording the reign of Nabonidus, last king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire</a>, and the Persian conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>. It notes repeated years in which the New Year festival was not held because the king was absent. It is a key source for the decline of the Chaldean Empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nabonidus-chronicle.jpg" alt="Nabonidus Chronicle" width="320" height="330" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nabonidus%27s_faults_and_absences_were_recorded_alongside_events_of_his_reign._By_the_autumn_of_539_BCE,_Babylon_had_surrendered_to_the_army_of_king_Cyrus_to_become_part_of_the_growing_Achaemenid_Empire._From_Babylon,_Iraq,_530-400_BCE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The chronicle belongs to the series of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-chronicles">Babylonian Chronicles</a>, which record year by year the accessions and deaths of kings, major campaigns and notable religious events, reporting only what mattered directly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>. It survives on a single clay tablet, 14 cm by 14 cm, written in two columns on each side and once holding some 300 to 400 lines, of which little more than 75 are still legible. The surviving copy was probably made by a scribe in the Seleucid period, but the original was probably written in the late 6th or early 5th century BCE, perhaps by the same scribe as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabonassar">Nabonassar</a> to Shamash-shum-ukin Chronicle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The text probably began with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabonidus">Nabonidus</a>'s accession in 556 BCE, and it mentions his campaigns against a place called Hume and in the West. In his sixth year it records <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a>'s plunder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecbatana">Ecbatana</a>, the capital of the Median king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astyages">Astyages</a>, and in several entries it describes the king's stay at the Arabian oasis of Tema. Another campaign of Cyrus in the ninth year may be his attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a>.</p><p>A long passage on the seventeenth and final year tells of the gods of other cities being brought into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, the Persian victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-opis">Battle of Opis</a>, the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sippar">Sippar</a> and Babylon without further fighting, and Cyrus's welcome and appointment of governors. It ends with mourning for a king's wife and a mention of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambyses-ii">Cambyses II</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a> bought the tablet in 1879 from the dealers Spartali &amp; Co. Its findspot is unknown, though it is presumed to have come from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>. Sir Henry Rawlinson first discussed it, as the Annals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabonidus">Nabonidus</a>, in the Athenaeum of 14 February 1880, and T. G. Pinches published the first English translation in 1882; Sidney Smith, A. Leo Oppenheim, Albert Kirk Grayson and others have translated it since.</p><p>Scholars see it as the work of the priests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marduk">Marduk</a> and part of a group of pro-Persian texts, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-cylinder">Cyrus Cylinder</a> and the Verse Account of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabonidus">Nabonidus</a>, that attack the king for religious infidelity. Julye Bidmead links the priests' hostility to his attempts to promote the moon god Sîn. Even so, Amélie Kuhrt calls it the most reliable and sober ancient account of the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nabonidus Chronicle” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Nabonidus Chronicle</b> records year after year in which the New Year festival, which required the king's presence, was not held.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>According to the Babylonian <b>Nabonidus Chronicle</b>, when Astyages marched against Cyrus around 550 BCE his army mutinied and handed him over, and Cyrus then seized Ecbatana and its treasure.</blockquote><a href="/empire/median-empire">Median Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
